{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-5370"] :question/worked-solution "Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124137"] :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a sum of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3! = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3! + 0! &= 6 + 1 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124138"] :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $2!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n2! = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2!}{0!} &= \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-19838"] :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $7! = 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4!$ and $3! = 3 \\cdot 2!$. Then, we can cancel the $4!$ and the $2!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7!3!}{4!2!} &= \\frac{(7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4!)(3 \\cdot 2!)}{4! \\cdot 2!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot \\cancel{4!})(3 \\cdot \\cancel{2!})}{\\cancel{4!} \\cdot \\cancel{2!}} \\\\\n&= (7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 630\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
